data: {"type": "thinking", "id": "t5", "content": "I'm looking at the social media data across platforms—LinkedIn has the strongest presence with 5,000 followers and 255 posts, while the other platforms are much smaller. Instagram is newer, only tracking data from May 2026, so it has fewer historical records. I'm starting to visualize this with a bar chart showing followers by platform.\n\nNow I'll create additional visualizations: a bar chart for post counts across platforms, a line chart tracking follower growth daily for each platform, metrics around engagement rates, and identifying which posts performed best."}

event: final
data: {"type": "action", "id": "act6", "icon": "chart", "title": "Creating chart", "results": [], "results_units": "Results", "started_at": "2026-08-01T09:02:30.263802Z", "ended_at": "2026-08-01T09:02:35.729817Z", "input": {"source": "\nimport plotly.express as px\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# 1. Followers by platform\nfig1 = px.bar(lifetime.sort_values('total_followers', ascending=False), x='platform', y='total_followers',\n              title='Total Followers by Platform', text='total_followers')\nfig1.update_traces(texttemplate='%{text:,.0f}', textposition='outside')\nfig1.show()\n\n# 2. Total posts by platform (lifetime, from lifetime stats table)\nfig2 = px.bar(lifetime.sort_values('total_posts', ascending=False), x='platform', y='total_posts',\n              title='Total Posts by Platform (Lifetime)', text='total_posts')\nfig2.update_traces(textposition='outside')\nfig2.show()\n\ndisplay(lifetime[['platform','total_followers','total_following','total_posts']])\n"}}

