data: {"type": "action", "id": "act20", "icon": "bigquery", "title": "Loaded BigQuery instructions", "results": [{"icon": "", "title": "Result"}], "results_units": "Results", "ended_at": "2026-08-01T09:08:35.413667Z", "output": "# BigQuery Toolkit\n\nThe user's BigQuery data warehouse: read-only SQL over their GCP project(s) — datasets, tables, and query results. Some marketing-relevant or order/revenue/cost tables may be available. Read-only (no INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE/DDL). Every query scans bytes and bills the project, so mind cost.\n\n## BigQuery specifics\n- Pass `projectId` (the project the query runs and bills in) to every call. Read tables in other accessible projects by fully-qualifying `project.dataset.table`.\n- Discover before you query: the catalog is the metadata tools (`list_dataset_ids`, `list_table_ids`, `get_dataset_info`, `get_table_info`) plus `INFORMATION_SCHEMA` — read a table's schema before writing SQL; don't guess column names or types.\n- Cost discipline: BigQuery bills by bytes scanned, not rows returned. Always `LIMIT` exploratory queries, never `SELECT *` on a wide or large table (select only the columns you need), filter on the partition / clustering column, and prefer metadata or `INFORMATION_SCHEMA` over scanning data. Results are capped at a modest row count and runtime, so design queries to return aggregates, not raw dumps.\n\n## Reading & computing at scale\nUse direct tool calls for targeted reads (the schema, one record, a small list or count). For volume or exact computation — paging many records, joins (e.g. CRM revenue × ad spend), aggregations, cohorts, time-in-stage, dedup — work in the code interpreter, where this toolkit's tools are available as functions: return just the result instead of pulling raw rows into the conversation (or an exploration sub-agent, which searches and navigates the tool's resources).\n\n## PII and Contact details\nRedaction is defense-in-depth: recognized contact-detail columns and detectable email/phone values are masked before results reach the agent, but arbitrary aliases and unformatted values may not be identifiable. Never treat redaction as making raw PII queries safe. Prefer aggregates and computed metrics, operate over IDs and keys, and never bulk-pull raw customer rows into the conversation.\n\n## Record what you learn\nThe warehouse schema is customer-defined: which datasets and tables matter, what their columns mean, the right join keys, and the quirks that make queries correct live with the account, not in `INFORMATION_SCHEMA`. That knowledge belongs in the `DATA-WAREHOUSE.md` topic file (`/shared/Instructions/Topics/DATA-WAREHOUSE.md`), organized per project: read it before re-discovering the catalog. When this run confirms durable, account-wide warehouse knowledge — which datasets and tables matter, what a column means, a join key, a query quirk — record it there with the library tools so later sessions and teammates reuse it, and tell the user in one line what you saved. When the point is a judgment call the warehouse cannot settle on its own — whether a standing filter or exclusion should always apply, which of several overlapping tables is authoritative — confirm with the user before recording it. Never guess semantics or figures the warehouse doesn't hold — confirm them with the user.\n\n## Available tools\n- `list_dataset_ids`: List BigQuery dataset IDs in a Google Cloud project.\n- `get_dataset_info`: Get metadata information about a BigQuery dataset.\n- `list_table_ids`: List table ids in a BigQuery dataset.\n- `get_table_info`: Get metadata information about a BigQuery table.\n- `execute_sql_readonly`: Run a read-only SQL query in the project and return the result. Prefer this tool over\n\nBefore calling a tool, always get its complete definition.", "has_succeeded": true, "artifacts": []}

